note.p:
] ̣ι μου ὅτι ἔπεμψά σοι [: In all likelihood, the letter preceding ι is either κ or α. Although the ending -αι (for the vocatives κύριαί μου or ἀδελφαί μου)

For the interchange of ε and αι see Gignac 1976: 193.
                                may at first sight appear tempting, the combination κι is the stronger candidate. From a paleographical point of view, the ligature between the two letters does not appear straight, unlike other attestations of αι written by the hand of the scribe (cf. the αι of γυναικί, ἐξπουνγεύεσθαι and κ]αί). If -κι is correct, then a possible restoration for the beginning of l. 5 would be τῇ γυναι]κ̣ί μου, most likely in the sense of “tell my wife that I sent to you.”
